# Decision memo: keep the campaign running, but don't budget on its size yet

**To:** Advertiser PM / VP Growth · **From:** Product Data Science · **Data:** Criteo ad-incrementality test
({{ R.datasets.datasets[0].rows|int }} users, randomized 85% eligible for ads / 15% held out). Readout:
`reports/criteo_readout.html` (`expkit analyze --config experiments/criteo.yaml`).

## Recommendation

**Keep the campaign on.** Conversions go up under every way of estimating it. **But hold any budget increase until
we know why the test groups differ.** The platform's verdict is **{{ S.verdict }}** (rule {{ S.rule }}). HOLD means
*keep the current state*: the campaign keeps running, but don't scale it up and don't claim a lift number. It does not
mean "pause". The direction is solid; the magnitude is not.

| How the lift is estimated | Conversion lift | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|
| What a dashboard shows (raw difference) | {{ C.raw.rel_lift|spct }} | {{ C.raw.rel_ci[0]|spct }} to {{ C.raw.rel_ci[1]|spct }} |
| Adjusted for pre-campaign user features (CUPAC) | {{ C.adjusted.rel_lift|spct }} | {{ C.adjusted.rel_ci[0]|spct }} to {{ C.adjusted.rel_ci[1]|spct }} |
| Adjusted, re-weighting non-visitors ×20 (if the published sample under-kept non-visitors) | {{ B.negative_sampling_sensitivity[-1].conversion_poststrat_relative|spct }} | ±{{ (100 * 1.96 * B.negative_sampling_sensitivity[-1].conversion_poststrat_relative_se)|f(1) }} pp (to {{ B.sensitivity_interval.k20_upper_ci_conversion|spct(0) }}) |

The honest planning range is **{{ ([C.adjusted.rel_lift, B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative[0]]|min)|spct(0) }} to
{{ B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative[1]|spct(0) }}** (with sampling error up to {{ B.sensitivity_interval.k20_upper_ci_conversion|spct(0) }}), not a single number.
These levels are not a plausible real ad effect. Criteo distorted the released sample on purpose so that "the original
incrementality level cannot be deduced". They are useful for the decision's direction, and for the method.

## Why the number is uncertain

The groups have the right sizes (85.000% / 15.000%), but not the right *mix*: among the 10% of users most likely to
visit, {{ B.deciles_by_prognostic_score[9].treatment_share_all|pct }} are in the ad group instead of 85%. The dataset's
documentation describes two processing steps that could cause this, and they imply different corrections. Until data
engineering tells us which one applied, any single lift number is an assumption. What does not depend on the
assumption: the effect is positive, and its odds ratio is about {{ B.odds_ratios.conversion.mantel_haenszel_or|f(2) }}–{{ B.odds_ratios.conversion.crude_or|f(2) }}.

| If engineering finds… | Then plan on | Action |
|---|---|---|
| per-test re-balancing residue (assignment depended on user features) | the feature-adjusted lift, {{ C.adjusted.rel_lift|spct(0) }} | keep the campaign; budget on the adjusted number |
| visit-label negative sampling at a known rate | re-weight by that rate: up to {{ B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative[1]|spct(0) }} | keep; re-run the readout with the real rate |
| conversion-label negative sampling | about {{ B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative_if_conversion_label_sampled[0]|spct(0) }} to {{ B.sensitivity_interval.conversion_poststrat_relative_if_conversion_label_sampled[1]|spct(0) }} | keep; re-run with the real rate |

In every branch: keep the campaign as long as cost per exposed user < value per conversion × {{ E.cace|pp(2) }}.

Caveat on the evidence. The data do not fit pure outcome sampling, because the ad group's share also rises among
*non-visitors* in the top decile. That argument assumes ads never *lower* anyone's visit propensity, and it rests on a
single decile (z = {{ B.explanations.observed_nonvisitor_share_top_decile_z|f(1) }}).

## Is it worth the money? (prices are assumptions; we have no cost data)

