## Where to spend

If we only show ads to the 20% of users the model ranks highest, we keep **{{ D.top20.conv_x_unweighted.share_of_incremental|pct(0) }}–{{ D.top20.conv_x.share_of_incremental|pct(0) }}**
of the extra conversions, depending on adjustment (unweighted vs propensity-weighted; the weighted figure has a 90% CI of
{{ D.top20.conv_x.share_of_incremental_ci90[0]|pct(0) }}–{{ D.top20.conv_x.share_of_incremental_ci90[1]|pct(0) }} and assumes selection on observables)
while buying **{{ D.top20.conv_x.share_of_exposures|pct(0) }}** of today's ad exposures. The exposures we would cut
deliver conversions at about {{ D.top20.conv_x.tail_marginal_efficiency|f(2) }}× the average rate, so cutting them pays off
whenever the campaign's average iROAS is below about {{ (1 / D.top20.conv_x.tail_marginal_efficiency)|f(1) }}. A simpler "who is likely to
convert" model does even better on this data ({{ D.top20.conv_resp.share_of_incremental|pct(0) }} on
{{ D.top20.conv_resp.share_of_exposures|pct(0) }}), because the ads already mostly reach high-intent users. Test the targeting policy
against today's policy in a holdout before moving budget.

## Next steps

1. **Data integrity (this week).** Ask engineering how the released sample was built: per-test re-balancing vs.
   label-dependent negative sampling. That decides which number in the table above is right.
2. **Log counterfactual exposure in the control group** (ghost ads). It would detect the same effect with about
   {{ NC.visit.dilution.n_ratio_itt_over_ideal_trigger_weighted_85_15|f(0) }}× fewer users for visits and measure "the effect of seeing an ad" cleanly.
3. **Targeting holdout** for the top-{{ (100 * D.top20.conv_x.top)|f(0) }}% policy analysed above, with conversions as the primary
   metric and spend as the guardrail.
